import{_ as t,o as a,c as n,a2 as o}from"./chunks/framework.CDU0sSpB.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"Agent Reference Patterns: Pi, OpenClaw, Hermes, DeerFlow, and LoopX","description":"Patterns Hearth adopts or rejects for proactive service, desktop operation, memory, method evolution, multi-device use, and long-running operation.","frontmatter":{"title":"Agent Reference Patterns: Pi, OpenClaw, Hermes, DeerFlow, and LoopX","description":"Patterns Hearth adopts or rejects for proactive service, desktop operation, memory, method evolution, multi-device use, and long-running operation.","outline":[2,3]},"headers":[],"relativePath":"en/design/agent-reference-patterns.md","filePath":"en/design/agent-reference-patterns.md","lastUpdated":1793970154000}'),r={name:"en/design/agent-reference-patterns.md"};function i(s,e,d,l,c,p){return a(),n("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[o('<h1 id="agent-reference-patterns-pi-openclaw-hermes-deerflow-and-loopx" tabindex="-1">Agent Reference Patterns: Pi, OpenClaw, Hermes, DeerFlow, and LoopX <a class="header-anchor" href="#agent-reference-patterns-pi-openclaw-hermes-deerflow-and-loopx" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Agent Reference Patterns: Pi, OpenClaw, Hermes, DeerFlow, and LoopX&quot;">​</a></h1><p>Across Phases 3–6, Hearth does not invent device control, proactive service, memory, method evaluation, or long-running operation from scratch. We studied working open agents and adopted only the parts that reduce accidental input, unauthorized expansion, wrong memory, and irreversible change. These notes reflect a review of public OpenClaw, Hermes, DeerFlow, and LoopX implementation or design documentation on 2026-11-06.</p><p>This page records design references, not integration claims. Hearth does not import these projects&#39; runtimes, codebases, or protocol implementations. Pi influences the message loop and tool shape of the native agent kernel; DeerFlow and LoopX influence task decomposition, evaluation layering, and delivery method only.</p><h2 id="shared-conclusions" tabindex="-1">Shared conclusions <a class="header-anchor" href="#shared-conclusions" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Shared conclusions&quot;">​</a></h2><p>OpenClaw and Hermes separate connection, capability grants, action execution, and human takeover. A connected device is not an authorized device. A one-time approval is not a permanent grant. The model may reference the current observation only; it cannot hold long-lived credentials or silently fall back outside the approved capability and window scope. Phases 4–6 apply the same rule: a suggestion is not an execution, a candidate is not an adopted capability, and a backup file is not proof of a complete migration.</p><h2 id="openclaw-patterns-worth-adopting" tabindex="-1">OpenClaw patterns worth adopting <a class="header-anchor" href="#openclaw-patterns-worth-adopting" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;OpenClaw patterns worth adopting&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>Pairing is separate from capability approval.</strong> Pairing only establishes the connection. Declared commands or capabilities require another approval. Execution must satisfy declared, approved, and gateway-policy checks together.</li><li><strong>Deny rules win.</strong> Extending capabilities requires a new approval, and revoking a device immediately invalidates its grants.</li><li><strong>Short-lived pairing, durable capability records.</strong> Pairing requests expire quickly. No capability is available before approval.</li><li><strong>Observation is distinct from action.</strong> Screen snapshots carry a frame ID, dimensions, and execution ID. Coordinate references expire. Desktop actions use <code>computer.act</code>; observation uses <code>screen.snapshot</code>.</li><li><strong>Takeover precedes resumption.</strong> Human takeover pauses agent input and cancels pending input. The agent must explicitly take control again and observe anew before resuming.</li><li><strong>Fail closed.</strong> If observation fails, the control provider changes, or a request is out of scope, return a structured refusal rather than silently choosing another path.</li><li><strong>Proactive suggestions are separate from execution.</strong> Proactive mode first produces an explainable suggestion. Accepting it or converting it to a task still follows the normal task and approval chain; being “proactive” grants no extra authority.</li></ul><h2 id="hermes-patterns-worth-adopting" tabindex="-1">Hermes patterns worth adopting <a class="header-anchor" href="#hermes-patterns-worth-adopting" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Hermes patterns worth adopting&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>Explicit permission modes.</strong> <code>standard</code>, <code>bounded</code>, and <code>unrestricted</code> are fixed at startup. <code>bounded</code> requires one approval of a capability manifest and fails closed on out-of-scope actions.</li><li><strong>Native Windows input.</strong> UIAutomation plus <code>SendInput</code>/<code>PostMessage</code> drive actions while avoiding unnecessary foreground-focus changes.</li><li><strong>Short-lived references.</strong> Element references belong to the current screenshot or observation generation and fail clearly when stale.</li><li><strong>Independent health checks.</strong> A <code>doctor</code> path exposes missing drivers, insufficient permissions, and Session 0 problems before a task half-executes.</li><li><strong>Credentials remain in the main process.</strong> Device connection credentials are not exposed to the renderer or ordinary plugins.</li><li><strong>Cancellation and no silent downgrade.</strong> Cancellation is explicit, and the capability manifest cannot change silently during a run.</li><li><strong>Diagnosable runtime state.</strong> Health checks should run independently of tasks and expose the runtime environment, permissions, and latest maintenance state early. Hearth extends this into health snapshots, maintenance records, and redacted diagnostics exports.</li></ul><h2 id="pi-patterns-worth-adopting" tabindex="-1">Pi patterns worth adopting <a class="header-anchor" href="#pi-patterns-worth-adopting" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Pi patterns worth adopting&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>A small, explicit single-agent loop.</strong> Model turn, tool call, tool result, next turn. Hearth does not start with complex multi-agent orchestration.</li><li><strong>Messages are state.</strong> System prompts, user input, assistant tool calls, and tool results are stored in order. Recovery continues the same sequence instead of rebuilding a prompt.</li><li><strong>Tools are capability boundaries.</strong> Reading, writing, shell, network, and user questions have separate task permissions. The model cannot expand them mid-run.</li><li><strong>Recoverable execution.</strong> Checkpoints are written after turns and tool steps. Waiting input, budget exhaustion, and service restart resume from an explicit position.</li><li><strong>A compact completion signal.</strong> A completion tool declares the result, and the system verifies artifacts. The model saying “done” is not completion.</li></ul><p>Hearth implements these principles in its own <code>@hearth/agent-runtime</code> without importing the Pi npm package. The native executor handles general tasks; Codex remains responsible for repository and engineering work. Both share task state, permissions, budgets, and the artifact contract.</p><h2 id="deerflow-patterns-worth-adopting" tabindex="-1">DeerFlow patterns worth adopting <a class="header-anchor" href="#deerflow-patterns-worth-adopting" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;DeerFlow patterns worth adopting&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>Task decomposition and staged evidence.</strong> Break a complex task into inspectable stages, then let later steps reference prior evidence instead of handing the entire goal to one opaque execution.</li><li><strong>Separate research, generation, and evaluation.</strong> Candidate production and candidate evaluation can be separate, so one run does not both change a method and grade its own change.</li></ul><p>Hearth borrows only this decomposition, evidence, and layered-evaluation structure for method-candidate generation and baseline/trial comparison. It does not integrate DeerFlow or add a multi-agent collaboration runtime.</p><h2 id="loopx-patterns-worth-adopting" tabindex="-1">LoopX patterns worth adopting <a class="header-anchor" href="#loopx-patterns-worth-adopting" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;LoopX patterns worth adopting&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>Small closed-loop delivery.</strong> Converge one verifiable capability change at a time instead of packaging several unverified features into one “big completion”.</li><li><strong>State and evidence together.</strong> A delivery note records verified paths, explicit gaps, and claims that are not allowed. A page or passing test does not replace real acceptance.</li><li><strong>Acceptance by boundary.</strong> High-risk capabilities such as devices, backup, and long-running operation require their own failure-path drills, not only a happy-path demonstration.</li></ul><p>Hearth uses LoopX only as a delivery-method reference. It is not a runtime dependency, and its workflow mechanics are not embedded in the Hearth scheduler.</p><h2 id="hearth-adoption-boundary" tabindex="-1">Hearth adoption boundary <a class="header-anchor" href="#hearth-adoption-boundary" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Hearth adoption boundary&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Decision</th><th>Hearth behavior</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Device identity</td><td>A same-machine Windows helper creates a short-lived pairing request over <code>127.0.0.1</code>. The user enters its pairing code in Hearth. The device token remains in the helper and server only.</td></tr><tr><td>Capability approval</td><td>Successful pairing grants nothing. Observation, desktop control, and UI automation are approved separately and bound to an application/window allowlist. Revocation is immediate.</td></tr><tr><td>Observation reference</td><td>Every observation returns a snapshot with frame ID, dimensions, and timestamp. Actions carry the frame ID; the server rechecks device presence, capability, controller, and observation generation before dispatch.</td></tr><tr><td>Human takeover</td><td>Pause, resume, takeover, release, and stop are first-class states. Takeover stops dispatch and asks the helper to cancel pending input. Release requires a new observation.</td></tr><tr><td>Action granularity</td><td>Only discrete low-risk actions inside allowlisted applications, such as click, short text entry, and key presses. Every action returns before/after observation evidence and a structured receipt.</td></tr><tr><td>Data retention</td><td>Screenshots stay in the local device data directory with short expiry. The server records audit events, not the full screen, in the event log.</td></tr><tr><td>Failure handling</td><td>Missing drivers, insufficient permissions, lost windows, scope mismatch, or unverifiable results stop the flow and return control to the user. High-risk actions are not retried, and success is never fabricated.</td></tr><tr><td>Proactive service</td><td>Produce only suggestions with source, why-now, suggested action, impact, and risk. Accept records a decision; convert creates a queued task; external actions still require approval. Daily limits, quiet hours, cooldown, and pause constrain interruption.</td></tr><tr><td>Memory</td><td>Memory starts as a candidate and needs user confirmation. Recall records reasons and budgets; injected context is marked untrusted background. Sensitive memory is not recalled by default, <code>secret</code> is never recalled, and forgetting redacts projections, versions, events, and recall audits.</td></tr><tr><td>Method evolution</td><td>A candidate can only inherit the intersection of source-task capabilities. Trials read existing terminal traces and create no external side effects. Only repeatable improvement evidence permits promotion, with rejection, rollback, and reasons retained.</td></tr><tr><td>Multi-device execution</td><td>Task leases use owner, TTL, and epoch. Expired takeover advances the epoch and rejects the old executor&#39;s commits. Sync copies only filtered event projections, not approvals, sources, outbox, device, or desktop-control events.</td></tr><tr><td>Revocation and migration</td><td>Device capabilities, device tokens, and sync peers can be revoked or rotated. The global kill switch blocks approvals and scheduler restart. Old-device invalidation needs a real drill, and database restore is described separately from artifact migration.</td></tr><tr><td>Backup and restore</td><td>Use a passphrase-encrypted database archive. Validate format, schema, integrity, and columns against a temporary database before transactional replacement, roll back on failure, and explicitly exclude artifact bodies, workspaces, attachments, and screenshots.</td></tr><tr><td>Long-running operation</td><td>Health, maintenance, and diagnostics are first-class. Maintenance cleans expired operational state without deleting tasks, memories, or artifacts. A failed maintenance run degrades health and never relaxes approval as “automatic repair”.</td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="out-of-scope-for-this-phase" tabindex="-1">Out of scope for this phase <a class="header-anchor" href="#out-of-scope-for-this-phase" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Out of scope for this phase&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li>Public remote device access, shared multi-user devices, or cross-device screen streaming.</li><li>Global keyboard and mouse control outside the approved window allowlist.</li><li>Password managers, system security settings, payments, and irreversible file operations.</li><li>Sending screenshots automatically to chat channels or exposing device tokens to the renderer.</li><li>Pretending a real Windows helper was verified. Anything not testable in the current environment remains disabled and is listed in delivery status.</li><li>Turning proactive suggestions into an auto-executor or skipping approval because the system originated the suggestion.</li><li>Treating candidate memory as fact or a candidate method as an adopted capability.</li><li>Claiming a live shadow executor, phone PWA/push/remote takeover, or a complete migratable backup.</li><li>Describing DeerFlow or LoopX as integrated components. They provide design and delivery-method references only.</li><li>Describing Pi as an integrated dependency. Hearth uses its single-agent kernel shape as a reference and provides its own TypeScript implementation.</li></ul><h2 id="impact-by-phase" tabindex="-1">Impact by phase <a class="header-anchor" href="#impact-by-phase" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Impact by phase&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>Phase 2:</strong> Drafts and approvals are separate, approvals are short-lived and single-use, approved effects write only to the local outbox rather than pretending to send mail, duplicate confirmations do not duplicate writes, and failures distinguish “not written” from “outcome uncertain”.</li><li><strong>Phase 3:</strong> Device pairing, capability approval, observation frames, human takeover, and control epochs are modeled separately. Actions that cannot be observed or verified are refused rather than silently downgraded.</li><li><strong>Phase 4:</strong> Proactive suggestions provide decision material only. Accept, ignore, snooze, and convert are first-class actions. Attention budgets, quiet hours, and pause take precedence over “more reminders”.</li><li><strong>Phase 5:</strong> Memory and methods start as candidates; recall and method use retain source and reason. Forgetting, rejection, and rollback are verifiable first-class paths.</li><li><strong>Phase 6:</strong> Sync, leases, revocation, backup restore, health, and maintenance default to fail-closed or rollback. A happy path does not replace lost-device, offline, or multi-week drills.</li></ul>',24)])])}const g=t(r,[["render",i]]);export{u as __pageData,g as default};
